window.PROJECTS = [{"id":"students","title":"Student marks analytics","level":"Beginner","minutes":35,"dataset":"students","story":"A college wants to understand its exam results. You get a table of 600 students with exam marks, attendance and study hours - and, like real data, some marks are missing (nan). Use slicing, boolean masks, statistics and sorting to answer the dean's questions.","learn":["slicing columns","nan-aware statistics","boolean masks","argsort / ranking","np.digitize bins","axis arguments","correlation","standardising"],"prelude":"# Provided for you (do not change):\n# students = 600 x 11 array. Columns: 0 student_id, 1 age, 2 gender (0=M, 1=F), 3 height_cm, 4 weight_kg,\n#            5 math, 6 physics, 7 chemistry, 8 english, 9 attendance_pct, 10 study_hours   (blank cells are nan)\nids = students[:, 0]\ngender = students[:, 2]\nmarks = students[:, 5:9]          # the four subject columns, shape (600, 4)\nattendance = students[:, 9]\nstudy = students[:, 10]","steps":[{"title":"How many students have a missing mark?","task":"Some of the four subject marks are blank (nan). Count the students (rows of `marks`) that have AT LEAST ONE missing mark. Store the integer in `answer`.","starter":"answer = ...   # TODO: how many rows of marks contain at least one nan?","solution":"answer = int(np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1).sum())","hint":"np.isnan(marks) gives a True/False table. .any(axis=1) asks 'is any value in this ROW True?'. Summing the resulting booleans counts the rows.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-1","n":1,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"int64","shape":[],"data":72,"nan":null},"expected_text":"72","shape":[],"dtype":"int64"},{"title":"Average mark per subject","task":"Compute the average of each subject (math, physics, chemistry, english), ignoring missing values. Round to 2 decimals. `answer` is an array of 4 numbers.","starter":"answer = ...   # TODO: column means that skip nan, then round","solution":"answer = np.round(np.nanmean(marks, axis=0), 2)","hint":"np.nanmean is the nan-skipping mean. axis=0 gives one result per COLUMN. Wrap with np.round(..., 2).","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-2","n":2,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[4],"data":[56.14,51.36,52.76,59.6],"nan":[false,false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[56.14, 51.36, 52.76, 59.6 ]","shape":[4],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Fill the gaps","task":"Replace every missing mark by the average of ITS subject (from the previous step, unrounded). Call the 600x4 result `filled`, and set `answer = np.round(filled[rows][:5], 2)` where rows = the students that had a missing mark (the first five of them).","starter":"means = ...    # TODO: per-subject means ignoring nan\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\n# TODO: put the right mean into every nan cell of filled\nanswer = np.round(filled[rows][:5], 2)","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\nanswer = np.round(filled[rows][:5], 2)","hint":"np.where(np.isnan(filled)) returns the row numbers and column numbers of the missing cells. means[c] picks the matching subject mean for each missing cell, so filled[r, c] = means[c] fills them all at once.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-3","n":3,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[5,4],"data":[[51.0,45.0,47.0,59.6],[65.0,31.0,45.0,59.6],[56.14,65.0,72.0,70.0],[69.0,56.0,52.76,66.0],[56.14,60.0,55.0,60.0]],"nan":[[false,false,false,false],[false,false,false,false],[false,false,false,false],[false,false,false,false],[false,false,false,false]]},"expected_text":"[[51.  , 45.  , 47.  , 59.6 ],\n [65.  , 31.  , 45.  , 59.6 ],\n [56.14, 65.  , 72.  , 70.  ],\n [69.  , 56.  , 52.76, 66.  ],\n [56.14, 60.  , 55.  , 60.  ]]","shape":[5,4],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Top 5 students","task":"Using the `filled` marks (build them again below), compute each student's TOTAL of the four subjects. Find the student_ids of the 5 highest totals, best first. If two totals are equal the student that appears first in the table wins (use a stable sort). `answer` = array of 5 ids.","starter":"# first rebuild `filled` (copy from the previous step)\nmeans = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\ntotal = ...    # TODO: sum across the 4 subjects\n# TODO: positions of the 5 largest totals (stable), then look up ids\nanswer = ...","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\ntotal = filled.sum(axis=1)\norder = np.argsort(-total, kind='stable')[:5]\nanswer = ids[order]","hint":"argsort sorts ascending, so sort the NEGATED totals to get the largest first. kind='stable' keeps the original order for ties. Use the order to index into ids.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-4","n":4,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[5],"data":[1342.0,1109.0,1494.0,1528.0,1089.0],"nan":[false,false,false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[1342., 1109., 1494., 1528., 1089.]","shape":[5],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Letter grades","task":"Average each student's four filled marks. Grade bands: A = 80 or more, B = 65 to under 80, C = 50 to under 65, D = under 50. `answer` = how many students got [A, B, C, D] (array of 4 counts).","starter":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\n# TODO: assign a band number 0..3 to each student, then count each band\nanswer = ...","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\nband = np.digitize(avg, [50, 65, 80])        # 0=D, 1=C, 2=B, 3=A\ncounts = np.bincount(band, minlength=4)\nanswer = counts[::-1]","hint":"np.digitize(avg, [50, 65, 80]) returns 0 for <50, 1 for 50-65, 2 for 65-80, 3 for >=80. np.bincount counts each number; reverse it to get A first.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-5","n":5,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"int64","shape":[4],"data":[6,93,322,179],"nan":null},"expected_text":"[  6,  93, 322, 179]","shape":[4],"dtype":"int64"},{"title":"Pass rate","task":"A student PASSES when ALL four filled marks are at least 40. What percentage of the 600 students pass? Round to 1 decimal. `answer` = a single number.","starter":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\n# TODO: a boolean per student: is every mark >= 40?\nanswer = ...","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\npassed = (filled >= 40).all(axis=1)\nanswer = round(float(passed.mean() * 100), 1)","hint":"(filled >= 40) is a True/False table; .all(axis=1) is True only if the whole row is True. The mean of booleans is the fraction of True.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-6","n":6,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[],"data":76.0,"nan":false},"expected_text":"76.0","shape":[],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Do boys and girls score differently?","task":"Using the filled marks, compute each student's average mark, then the mean of those averages for gender 0 (M) and gender 1 (F). Round to 2 decimals. `answer` = array [mean_M, mean_F].","starter":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\n# TODO: use boolean masks gender == 0 and gender == 1\nanswer = ...","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\nanswer = np.round(np.array([avg[gender == 0].mean(), avg[gender == 1].mean()]), 2)","hint":"A mask like gender == 0 selects the rows of boys. avg[mask].mean() averages only those.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-7","n":7,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[2],"data":[55.89,54.12],"nan":[false,false]},"expected_text":"[55.89, 54.12]","shape":[2],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Does studying help?","task":"Some study_hours are missing. Keep only students whose study_hours is NOT nan, and compute the correlation coefficient between their study_hours and their average filled mark. Round to 4 decimals. `answer` = a single number.","starter":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\n# TODO: mask of students with a known study_hours, then np.corrcoef on the two arrays\nanswer = ...","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\nok = ~np.isnan(study)\nanswer = round(float(np.corrcoef(study[ok], avg[ok])[0, 1]), 4)","hint":"np.corrcoef(x, y) returns a 2x2 matrix; the correlation is the off-diagonal element [0, 1]. Remove nan rows first with a mask.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-8","n":8,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[],"data":0.3243,"nan":false},"expected_text":"0.3243","shape":[],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Standardise the marks","task":"Turn each SUBJECT column of `filled` into z-scores: (value - column mean) / column standard deviation (population std, the NumPy default). `answer` = the first 3 rows rounded to 3 decimals (a 3x4 array).","starter":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\n# TODO: z = (filled - mean) / std, computed per column\nanswer = ...","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\nz = (filled - filled.mean(axis=0)) / filled.std(axis=0)\nanswer = np.round(z[:3], 3)","hint":"Broadcasting: a (600,4) array minus a (4,) array subtracts each column's own mean. Use axis=0 for the statistics.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-9","n":9,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[3,4],"data":[[-2.53,-1.828,-1.53,-1.403],[0.996,-0.357,1.324,0.371],[-1.019,0.297,0.59,-0.051]],"nan":[[false,false,false,false],[false,false,false,false],[false,false,false,false]]},"expected_text":"[[-2.53 , -1.828, -1.53 , -1.403],\n [ 0.996, -0.357,  1.324,  0.371],\n [-1.019,  0.297,  0.59 , -0.051]]","shape":[3,4],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Top 10 percent","task":"Count the students whose average filled mark is at or above the 90th percentile of all averages (np.percentile with the default method). `answer` = integer count.","starter":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\n# TODO\nanswer = ...","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\nanswer = int((avg >= np.percentile(avg, 90)).sum())","hint":"cut = np.percentile(avg, 90) gives the threshold. Compare with >= and count with sum().","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-10","n":10,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"int64","shape":[],"data":61,"nan":null},"expected_text":"61","shape":[],"dtype":"int64"},{"title":"Attendance matters?","task":"Ignore students whose attendance is missing. Compare the average of the student averages for attendance BELOW 70 and attendance 70 AND ABOVE. Round to 2 decimals. `answer` = array [mean_below_70, mean_70_or_more].","starter":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\n# TODO: two masks that also exclude nan attendance\nanswer = ...","solution":"means = np.nanmean(marks, axis=0)\nfilled = marks.copy()\nrows = np.isnan(marks).any(axis=1)\nr, c = np.where(np.isnan(filled))\nfilled[r, c] = means[c]\navg = filled.mean(axis=1)\nok = ~np.isnan(attendance)\nlow = ok & (attendance < 70)\nhigh = ok & (attendance >= 70)\nanswer = np.round(np.array([avg[low].mean(), avg[high].mean()]), 2)","hint":"Comparisons with nan are False, so attendance >= 70 already excludes nan, but attendance < 70 also does - still, make the nan test explicit with ~np.isnan to be safe.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"students-11","n":11,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[2],"data":[53.82,55.03],"nan":[false,false]},"expected_text":"[53.82, 55.03]","shape":[2],"dtype":"float64"}]},{"id":"sensor","title":"Sensor data cleaning & anomaly detection","level":"Intermediate","minutes":45,"dataset":"sensor","story":"A weather station logs temperature, humidity, pressure and vibration every minute. The raw log contains blanks, an error code (-999), a power cut and a few suspicious spikes. Clean the data, smooth it, find the anomalies and summarise each day - all with vectorised NumPy.","learn":["sentinel values","np.interp gap filling","moving average with convolve","np.diff and runs","bincount group-by","integer arithmetic on time","masks"],"prelude":"# Provided for you (do not change). Every column is a COPY so you can modify it freely.\n# sensor columns: 0 t_sec (seconds since start), 1 temperature_c, 2 humidity_pct, 3 pressure_hpa, 4 vibration_g\nt, temp, hum, pres, vib = (sensor[:, i].copy() for i in range(5))","steps":[{"title":"How long is the recording?","task":"`t` holds seconds since the start (one reading per minute). How many DAYS does the log cover from the first to the last reading? Round to 3 decimals. `answer` = a number.","starter":"answer = ...   # TODO","solution":"answer = round(float((t[-1] - t[0]) / 86400), 3)","hint":"The span is last minus first, in seconds. A day has 24 * 60 * 60 = 86400 seconds.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-1","n":1,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[],"data":6.944,"nan":false},"expected_text":"6.944","shape":[],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Count the error codes","task":"Humidity uses -999 as an 'error' code. How many readings equal exactly -999? `answer` = integer.","starter":"answer = ...   # TODO","solution":"answer = int(np.count_nonzero(hum == -999))","hint":"(hum == -999) is a boolean array; np.count_nonzero (or sum) counts the True values. nan never equals anything, so blanks are not counted.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-2","n":2,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"int64","shape":[],"data":40,"nan":null},"expected_text":"40","shape":[],"dtype":"int64"},{"title":"Missing values per column","task":"First replace every -999 in `hum` by nan. Then count the nan values in each of temp, hum, pres, vib. `answer` = array of 4 integers (in that order).","starter":"# TODO: replace -999 by nan in hum\nanswer = ...","solution":"hum[hum == -999] = np.nan\nanswer = np.array([np.isnan(a).sum() for a in (temp, hum, pres, vib)])","hint":"Assign through a mask: hum[hum == -999] = np.nan. Then np.isnan(column).sum() for every column.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-3","n":3,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"int64","shape":[4],"data":[189,190,150,150],"nan":null},"expected_text":"[189, 190, 150, 150]","shape":[4],"dtype":"int64"},{"title":"Repair the temperature gaps","task":"Fill every missing temperature by LINEAR interpolation between the nearest valid readings (np.interp over the reading number). Call the result `temp_f`. `answer` = temp_f[5995:6005] rounded to 3 decimals - this slice covers the 40-minute power cut that starts at reading 6000.","starter":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\n# TODO: temp_f = interpolate at ALL positions using only the valid points\nanswer = ...","solution":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\nanswer = np.round(temp_f[5995:6005], 3)","hint":"np.interp(x_new, x_known, y_known): x_new = every index, x_known = idx[ok], y_known = temp[ok].","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-4","n":4,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[10],"data":[19.87,19.9,20.16,20.13,20.52,20.538,20.557,20.575,20.593,20.611],"nan":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[19.87 , 19.9  , 20.16 , 20.13 , 20.52 , 20.538, 20.557, 20.575, 20.593, 20.611]","shape":[10],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Smooth with a moving average","task":"Smooth `temp_f` (rebuild it below) with a 15-minute moving average using np.convolve(..., mode='valid'). `answer` = the first 5 values of the smoothed series, rounded to 3 decimals.","starter":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\n# TODO: convolve with a window of fifteen values that each equal 1/15\nanswer = ...","solution":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\nsmooth = np.convolve(temp_f, np.ones(15) / 15, mode='valid')\nanswer = np.round(smooth[:5], 3)","hint":"np.ones(15) / 15 is a box filter. mode='valid' only keeps positions where all 15 values exist, so the output is 14 shorter.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-5","n":5,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[5],"data":[17.937,17.967,17.953,17.955,17.933],"nan":[false,false,false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[17.937, 17.967, 17.953, 17.955, 17.933]","shape":[5],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Find sudden jumps","task":"A reading is suspicious when the temperature jumps by MORE than 3 degrees compared with the previous minute. Using `temp_f`, how many such jumps (consecutive pairs) are there? `answer` = integer.","starter":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\n# TODO: absolute differences of consecutive readings, count those > 3\nanswer = ...","solution":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\nanswer = int((np.abs(np.diff(temp_f)) > 3).sum())","hint":"np.diff(temp_f) is the change between neighbours. Take np.abs, compare with 3, and sum the booleans. One spike usually produces TWO jumps (up and back).","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-6","n":6,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"int64","shape":[],"data":44,"nan":null},"expected_text":"44","shape":[],"dtype":"int64"},{"title":"Temperature vs humidity","task":"Replace -999 in humidity by nan first. Using only the readings where BOTH `temp` and `hum` are valid (not nan), compute the correlation coefficient between them. Round to 3 decimals. `answer` = a number.","starter":"hum[hum == -999] = np.nan\n# TODO: mask of rows where neither is nan, then np.corrcoef\nanswer = ...","solution":"hum[hum == -999] = np.nan\nok = ~np.isnan(temp) & ~np.isnan(hum)\nanswer = round(float(np.corrcoef(temp[ok], hum[ok])[0, 1]), 3)","hint":"Combine two masks with &. np.corrcoef(...)[0, 1] is the correlation. It is strongly negative: warm air holds more moisture, so relative humidity falls.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-7","n":7,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[],"data":-0.967,"nan":false},"expected_text":"-0.967","shape":[],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Daily average temperature","task":"Group the readings by day (day number = t // 86400, integer division). Using the interpolated `temp_f`, compute the mean temperature of each day. Round to 2 decimals. `answer` = array with one value per day.","starter":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\nday = t // 86400\n# TODO: mean of temp_f for each distinct day number\nanswer = ...","solution":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\nday = (t // 86400).astype(int)\nsums = np.bincount(day, weights=temp_f)\ncounts = np.bincount(day)\nanswer = np.round(sums / counts, 2)","hint":"np.bincount(day, weights=values) adds up the values per day number; dividing by np.bincount(day) (the counts) gives the mean. day must be an integer array.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-8","n":8,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[7],"data":[22.01,22.0,22.02,22.0,22.0,21.99,22.26],"nan":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[22.01, 22.  , 22.02, 22.  , 22.  , 21.99, 22.26]","shape":[7],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Vibration alarms","task":"An ALARM is a run of consecutive readings with vibration above 1.0 g (ignore nan). Find the index at which each run STARTS. `answer` = array of start indices in order.","starter":"high = ...   # TODO: boolean array vib > 1.0\n# TODO: positions where high switches from False to True\nanswer = ...","solution":"high = (vib > 1.0)\nedges = np.diff(np.concatenate([[0], high.astype(int)]))\nanswer = np.where(edges == 1)[0]","hint":"Turn the mask into 0/1, put a 0 in front, and take np.diff: +1 marks a start (False -> True), -1 a stop. np.where(edges == 1)[0] gives the start positions.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-9","n":9,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"int64","shape":[12],"data":[284,587,1201,2327,3130,4123,4481,5853,6261,8378,8785,9528],"nan":null},"expected_text":"[ 284,  587, 1201, 2327, 3130, 4123, 4481, 5853, 6261, 8378, 8785, 9528]","shape":[12],"dtype":"int64"},{"title":"The hottest hour of the day","task":"Hour of day = (t // 3600) % 24. Using `temp_f`, find the hour (0-23) with the highest AVERAGE temperature. `answer` = integer.","starter":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\nhour = ...   # TODO\nanswer = ...","solution":"idx = np.arange(len(temp))\nok = ~np.isnan(temp)\ntemp_f = np.interp(idx, idx[ok], temp[ok])\nhour = ((t // 3600) % 24).astype(int)\nmean_by_hour = np.bincount(hour, weights=temp_f) / np.bincount(hour)\nanswer = int(np.argmax(mean_by_hour))","hint":"Same bincount trick as before, grouped by hour. np.argmax of the 24 averages is the hour number itself because the index equals the hour.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"sensor-10","n":10,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"int64","shape":[],"data":11,"nan":null},"expected_text":"11","shape":[],"dtype":"int64"}]},{"id":"regression","title":"Linear regression from scratch","level":"Advanced","minutes":50,"dataset":"houses","story":"Predict house prices from area, bedrooms and age using nothing but NumPy: explore the data, standardise it, solve the normal equation, measure the fit, then train the same model with gradient descent - the foundation of machine learning.","learn":["np.corrcoef","broadcasting","np.column_stack","np.linalg.solve","matrix @","gradient descent loop","train / test split","R-squared and RMSE"],"prelude":"# Provided for you (do not change). houses = 400 x 4 array: area_sqft, bedrooms, age_years, price_k (price in thousands)\narea, beds, age, price = houses.T\nfeatures = houses[:, :3]\ny = price","steps":[{"title":"Know your target","task":"Compute the mean and the (population) standard deviation of the price. Round both to 2 decimals. `answer` = array [mean, std].","starter":"answer = ...   # TODO","solution":"answer = np.round(np.array([price.mean(), price.std()]), 2)","hint":"price.mean() and price.std() - or np.mean / np.std. Put them in an array and round it.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-1","n":1,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[2],"data":[221.9,94.37],"nan":[false,false]},"expected_text":"[221.9 ,  94.37]","shape":[2],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Which features matter?","task":"Compute the correlation of each feature (area, bedrooms, age) with the price. Round to 3 decimals. `answer` = array of 3 numbers.","starter":"answer = ...   # TODO: np.corrcoef with each column of `features`","solution":"answer = np.round(np.array([np.corrcoef(features[:, j], y)[0, 1] for j in range(3)]), 3)","hint":"np.corrcoef(a, b)[0, 1] is the correlation of two vectors. Loop over the 3 columns with a list comprehension (small loops over columns are fine).","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-2","n":2,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[3],"data":[0.975,0.815,-0.185],"nan":[false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[ 0.975,  0.815, -0.185]","shape":[3],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Standardise the features","task":"Create Z = (features - column mean) / column std for all three feature columns (population std). `answer` = first 3 rows of Z rounded to 3 decimals (3x3 array).","starter":"answer = ...   # TODO","solution":"Z = (features - features.mean(axis=0)) / features.std(axis=0)\nanswer = np.round(Z[:3], 3)","hint":"Broadcasting subtracts the (3,) mean row from every row. Use axis=0.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-3","n":3,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[3,3],"data":[[-0.572,-0.331,0.428],[-1.427,-0.331,0.091],[-0.901,-0.331,0.361]],"nan":[[false,false,false],[false,false,false],[false,false,false]]},"expected_text":"[[-0.572, -0.331,  0.428],\n [-1.427, -0.331,  0.091],\n [-0.901, -0.331,  0.361]]","shape":[3,3],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Build the design matrix","task":"Create X by putting a column of ones in FRONT of the three features (the intercept column) so X has shape (400, 4). `answer` = X[0] (the first row).","starter":"answer = ...   # TODO: np.column_stack([ones, features])","solution":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\nanswer = X[0]","hint":"np.ones(len(features)) makes the column of ones; np.column_stack glues columns side by side.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-4","n":4,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[4],"data":[1.0,1541.0,2.0,32.0],"nan":[false,false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[   1., 1541.,    2.,   32.]","shape":[4],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Solve the normal equation","task":"Find the coefficients theta that minimise the squared error using the normal equation (X^T X) theta = X^T y with np.linalg.solve. `answer` = theta rounded to 4 decimals (intercept first, then area, bedrooms, age).","starter":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\n# TODO: theta = solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)\nanswer = ...","solution":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)\nanswer = np.round(theta, 4)","hint":"X.T @ X is a 4x4 matrix, X.T @ y a vector of 4 numbers. np.linalg.solve(A, b) solves A theta = b (better than inverting A).","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-5","n":5,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[4],"data":[30.9648,0.0899,12.1722,-0.8804],"nan":[false,false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[30.9648,  0.0899, 12.1722, -0.8804]","shape":[4],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Predict a new house","task":"Predict the price (in thousands) of a house with 2000 sq ft, 3 bedrooms and 10 years old, using theta from the normal equation. Round to 2 decimals. `answer` = a number.","starter":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)\nnew = np.array([1, 2000, 3, 10])   # leading 1 = intercept\n# TODO\nanswer = ...","solution":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)\nnew = np.array([1, 2000, 3, 10])\nanswer = round(float(new @ theta), 2)","hint":"The prediction is the dot product of the feature row (with the leading 1) and theta: new @ theta.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-6","n":6,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[],"data":238.53,"nan":false},"expected_text":"238.53","shape":[],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"How good is the fit? (R-squared)","task":"R-squared = 1 - SS_res / SS_tot, where SS_res = sum of squared residuals (y - prediction) and SS_tot = sum of squared deviations of y from its mean. Round to 4 decimals. `answer` = a number.","starter":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)\npred = X @ theta\n# TODO\nanswer = ...","solution":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)\npred = X @ theta\nss_res = ((y - pred) ** 2).sum()\nss_tot = ((y - y.mean()) ** 2).sum()\nanswer = round(float(1 - ss_res / ss_tot), 4)","hint":"Two sums of squares; their ratio is the share of variance NOT explained.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-7","n":7,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[],"data":0.975,"nan":false},"expected_text":"0.975","shape":[],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Prediction error (RMSE)","task":"Root mean squared error = sqrt(mean((y - pred)^2)). Round to 3 decimals. `answer` = a number (in thousands).","starter":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)\npred = X @ theta\n# TODO\nanswer = ...","solution":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)\npred = X @ theta\nanswer = round(float(np.sqrt(np.mean((y - pred) ** 2))), 3)","hint":"residuals = y - pred; square, mean, square-root.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-8","n":8,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[],"data":14.932,"nan":false},"expected_text":"14.932","shape":[],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Gradient descent","task":"Train the same model by gradient descent on STANDARDISED features: Z = standardised features (population std), Xs = [ones, Z]. Start with theta = zeros(4); repeat 500 times: theta = theta - 0.1 * (Xs.T @ (Xs @ theta - y)) / n. `answer` = theta rounded to 3 decimals.","starter":"Z = (features - features.mean(axis=0)) / features.std(axis=0)\nXs = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(Z)), Z])\nn = len(y)\ntheta = np.zeros(4)\n# TODO: loop 500 times\nanswer = np.round(theta, 3)","solution":"Z = (features - features.mean(axis=0)) / features.std(axis=0)\nXs = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(Z)), Z])\nn = len(y)\ntheta = np.zeros(4)\nfor _ in range(500):\n    grad = Xs.T @ (Xs @ theta - y) / n\n    theta = theta - 0.1 * grad\nanswer = np.round(theta, 3)","hint":"grad = Xs.T @ (Xs @ theta - y) / n is the gradient of half the mean squared error. After 500 small steps theta matches the closed-form answer on standardised data (the first number is the mean price).","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-9","n":9,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[4],"data":[221.9,80.475,13.684,-13.046],"nan":[false,false,false,false]},"expected_text":"[221.9  ,  80.475,  13.684, -13.046]","shape":[4],"dtype":"float64"},{"title":"Honest evaluation: train / test split","task":"Fit the normal equation using only the FIRST 320 rows (train) and compute the RMSE on the LAST 80 rows (test). Round to 3 decimals. `answer` = a number.","starter":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\nXtr, ytr = X[:320], y[:320]\nXte, yte = X[320:], y[320:]\n# TODO: fit on train, evaluate on test\nanswer = ...","solution":"X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(features)), features])\nXtr, ytr = X[:320], y[:320]\nXte, yte = X[320:], y[320:]\nth = np.linalg.solve(Xtr.T @ Xtr, Xtr.T @ ytr)\nanswer = round(float(np.sqrt(np.mean((yte - Xte @ th) ** 2))), 3)","hint":"Compute theta from the training rows only, then predict the test rows and compute the RMSE. A test error close to the training error means the model generalises.","rtol":1e-06,"atol":1e-06,"id":"regression-10","n":10,"expected":{"kind":"array","dtype":"float64","shape":[],"data":16.67,"nan":false},"expected_text":"16.67","shape":[],"dtype":"float64"}]}];
